  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nToday through next Saturday...\n\nDangerous heat will continue to worsen this weekend through early \nnext week. Near term guidance continues to shows a strong \nsubtropical ridge (596-598DM) across the Gulf Coast region and will \nremain in place through early next week. The increasingly warm \nthermal profiles, combined with the strong subsidence beneath the \nridge along with increased sunshine across the Gulf Coast states will \nlead to limited rain chances across portions of the Pine Belt. \nAlthough the some humidity will ease due to the strong subtropical \nridge, the combination of heat and humidity will ensure that most \ndays see peak heat index reading values in the 105-110 degree range \nthrough the weekend. At this time, no modifications have been made \nto the heat graphics for this weekend into early next week. \nLikewise, the areawide Heat Advisory remains in effect until 8 pm \nMonday evening. The worst timeframe is still looking to be late \nweekend into early next week (Sunday-Monday), where heat indices \ncould exceed 110F degrees. \n\nThere is a possibility that Extreme Heat Warnings could be \nintroduced to portions of our CWA as early as Sunday and extend into \nMonday and Tuesday. If an Extreme Heat Warning is introduced for \nSunday, it will likely start from the Delta and cover areas along \nand west of I-55. Heat trends will continued to be monitored as we \ngo through the weekend. The ongoing heat headlines will continue \nthrough the overnight periods with limited relief at night. Remember \nto stay cool and hydrated and check on vulnerable populations, \nand never leave children or pets unattended in hot vehicles as \nthese can become lethal, as quickly as a matter of minutes.\n\nAfternoon highs this weekend into early next week will peak in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across our area. Some mixing could occur, \nwhich could cause several locations to see air temperatures exceed \n100F degrees. Because of this, we could see some new record highs \n(and record lows) being set, especially if the warmer guidance \noverperforms. \n\nRain and storm chances remain limited through the period, with only \nperipheral areas in southeast MS seeing scarce showers and storm \ncoverage, mainly into early next week. Some relaxation of the stout \nridge will occur into early to mid next week. In conjunction with \nincreased deeper moisture, increased convergence from frontal zone \nsagging southward may cause some better rain coverage into early to \nmid next week. With dangerous heat, high humidity and steep lapse \nrates, will have to monitor robust storm potential as this type of \nthermal setup is typically supportive of robust diurnal microburst \nactivity. Mostly dry conditions will persist most areas through the \nweekend, outside of the Pine Belt. As the ridge gradually breaks \ndown into mid week, storm coverage will become scattered with highs \nand heat indices only a touch less oppressive. Heading into the \nweekend, rain chances across the area will remain low (between 15-\n20%) with slightly higher rain chances (around 25%) across the\nPine Belt. /CR/",
